    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\alpha=30^\\circ$, $h=10\\sqrt3\\ \\mathrm{m}$.\n\nPythagoras gives $OA=30\\ \\mathrm{m}$; then use $\\tan2\\alpha=3\\tan\\alpha$ and the tangent double-angle identity.",
      "worked": "In the right-angled ground triangle,\n$$\\begin{align*}OA^2&=(10\\sqrt{10})^2-10^2\\\\OA^2&=900\\\\OA&=30\\ \\mathrm{m}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe angles of elevation give\n$$\\begin{align*}\\tan\\alpha&=\\frac h{30}\\\\\\tan2\\alpha&=\\frac h{10}\\\\\\tan2\\alpha&=3\\tan\\alpha\\\\\\frac{2\\tan\\alpha}{1-\\tan^2\\alpha}&=3\\tan\\alpha.\\end{align*}$$\nSince the tower has positive height, $\\tan\\alpha>0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}2&=3(1-\\tan^2\\alpha)\\\\\\tan^2\\alpha&=\\frac13\\\\\\tan\\alpha&=\\frac1{\\sqrt3}\\\\\\alpha&=30^\\circ\\\\h&=30\\tan30^\\circ\\\\h&=10\\sqrt3\\ \\mathrm{m}.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
